637b802e8ec866e466f2a2efd7f39881a301cbcb perf(checkpoint): memoize the git common dir instead of forking git per write
resolveGitCommonDir spawns `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, and its two
callers run on per-turn paths: the push-discovery queue resolves it on
every checkpoint ref write, and the ephemeral store on every
shadow-branch write. Measured on an M4 Max, the fork is ~9.5ms of a
~12.9ms checkpoint write — 73% of the cost, and 4.5x once memoized.

A 30-day import of this repo's own Claude history is ~1,100 turns, so
`entire enable --import-history` was paying ~10s of pure fork/exec.

The value cannot change for a worktree's lifetime, so cache it by
worktree root. session.getGitCommonDir already caches this same value
process-wide for its own callers; this is its unmemoized twin. Only
successes are cached — a canceled context fails the subprocess
instantly, and caching that would poison every later caller.

